%S A401019 1,1,0,0,4,12,144,720,8640,100800,1209600,10886400,152409600,
%T A401019 1676505600,26824089600,448345497600,7846046208000,117690693120000,
%U A401019 2301506887680000,39125617090560000,845113329156096000,18976635663777792000,442788165488148480000
%N A401019 Number of permutations of {1..n} in which 1 is not adjacent to any prime.
%C A401019 Equivalently, number of permutations of {1..n} in which the product of any two adjacent elements is never a prime number.
%H A401019 Mykola Filipchuk, <a href="/A401019/b401019.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..450</a>
%F A401019 a(0) = a(1) = 1 and a(n) = (n - pi(n)) * (n - pi(n) - 1) * (n - 2)! for n >= 2, where pi = A000720.
%e A401019 For n = 4, a(4) = 4. The 4 valid permutations where 1 is adjacent only to 4 (since 2 and 3 are primes) are: (1, 4, 2, 3), (1, 4, 3, 2), (2, 3, 4, 1), (3, 2, 4, 1).
%t A401019 a[0]=a[1]=1; a[n_]:= (n - PrimePi[n]) * (n - PrimePi[n] - 1) * (n - 2)!; Array[a,23,0] (* _Stefano Spezia_, Oct 24 2026 *)
%o A401019 (Python)
%o A401019 import math
%o A401019 from sympy import primepi
%o A401019 def a(n):
%o A401019     if n <= 1: return 1
%o A401019     P = primepi(n)
%o A401019     return (n - P) * (n - P - 1) * math.factorial(n - 2)
%Y A401019 Cf. A000142, A000720, A062298.
%K A401019 nonn,easy,new
%O A401019 0,5
%A A401019 _Mykola Filipchuk_, Oct 13 2026
